  "answer_text": "Yes, parathyroid disease **can** cause neck pain, along with a wide range of other symptoms. Here's a detailed breakdown:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd34 Direct Neck-Related Symptoms\n\n- A common symptom of parathyroid disease is a **lump in the neck**, as parathyroid tumors can grow anywhere along the thyroid.\n- In some cases, patients present with **non-specific neck and shoulder pain**, and are later found to have elevated parathyroid hormone (PTH) levels and an irregular parathyroid gland on imaging.\n- In more serious cases, a condition called **parathyromatosis** \u2014 characterized by the seeding and growth of hyperfunctioning parathyroid tissue throughout the neck or mediastinum \u2014 can further complicate the condition.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\uddb4 Bone & Muscle Pain (Which Can Include the Neck)\n\n- The most common symptoms of hyperparathyroidism include **chronic fatigue, body aches, bone pain, difficulty sleeping, memory loss, poor concentration, depression, and headaches.**\n- Parathyroid disease can lead to **osteoporosis**, which can cause broken bones and fractures. If a fracture happens in the spine or neck, it could even result in more serious complications.\n- The possibility of parathyroid disease should be considered in the presence of **multiple pathological fractures, muscle atrophy, generalized bone pain, hypercalcemia, and a neck mass.**\n\n---\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f Other Serious Complications\n\n- Parathyroid disease also frequently leads to **osteoporosis, kidney stones, hypertension, cardiac arrhythmias, and kidney failure.**\n- Occasionally, one or more parathyroid glands may make too much hormone \u2014 a condition known as **primary hyperparathyroidism** \u2014 causing calcium to be pulled out of the bones and released into the blood, where it can cause numerous problems.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfe5 Treatment\n\n- There is no medication that can cure primary hyperparathyroidism. The only way to resolve the problem is with an **operation to remove the abnormal parathyroid gland(s).**\n- After successful surgery, patients often report significant relief \u2014 for example, one patient **no longer reported neck and shoulder pain** following her operation.\n\n---\n\n**In summary**, neck pain can be a symptom of parathyroid disease, either directly from a growing tumor or lump, or indirectly through bone loss and musculoskeletal effects. If you are experiencing unexplained neck pain along with other symptoms like fatigue, bone aches, or kidney stones, it is worth speaking to a doctor about getting your calcium and PTH levels checked.",
